{% extends 'layouts/page.njk' %}

{% block content %}
  <div class="govuk-grid-row">
    <div class="govuk-grid-column-two-thirds">
      <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">How Design help has been built</h1>

      <p class="govuk-body">
        Design help is mainly a tool for the Defra design team to record and score evidence for the GDaD (Government Digital and Data) pay framework. It grew out of an earlier directory that helps designers find and offer help to each other.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        This page explains how we built Design help in practice. It covers the tools we used, where it runs, what we changed for Defra, and how we used AI to help write the code. We wrote it so people who use or look after the tool can see how it was made. It is not a formal document for buying or auditing the service.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">How we used AI to write the code</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        We used <strong>AI-supported development</strong> for almost all of the coding. Early work happened in the <a class="govuk-link" href="https://cursor.com/">Cursor</a> editor. More recent work has been done with <a class="govuk-link" href="https://www.anthropic.com/claude-code">Claude Code</a>, an AI coding tool made by Anthropic.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The maintainer describes each change in plain language and directs the work. Separate AI helper agents then build each change and independently check it. Automated tests, which use made-up data, prove each change works. The maintainer tries each change before it is released and decides what to keep.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The maintainer is not a full-time developer. This way of working meant we could cover more in less time than we would expect from one person working only by hand.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The work was iterative and conversational. Some people call that informally &ldquo;vibe coding&rdquo;. The maintainer set the direction, tested what changed, and chose what to release.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        This approach is <strong>not</strong> the same as a full delivery team with strong checks for a high-scale public service. It <strong>is</strong> a deliberate test of how far we can get with modern AI tools when the audience is smaller and the risks are clearer.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">GOV.UK Frontend and accessibility</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The service started life in the <a class="govuk-link" href="https://prototype-kit.service.gov.uk/docs">GOV.UK Prototype Kit</a> and has since been rebuilt on Defra&rsquo;s Core Delivery Platform service templates, which also use <strong>GOV.UK Frontend</strong> and patterns people already know from GOV.UK services.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        <strong>Accessibility</strong> matters. We want journeys to be clear and easy to use, including for people who use assistive technology. GOV.UK Frontend does not by itself prove we meet every WCAG success criterion. It does give a solid starting point: sensible page structure, typography, visible focus, and many components that are built with accessibility in mind.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        We kept Defra-specific styling light so we were less likely to weaken that starting point by accident.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Defra branding and layout</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        This version of the service is <strong>Defra-only</strong> in its look and wording. We changed the default <strong>header</strong>, built a custom <strong>footer</strong>, and adjusted the navigation strip, favicon, and colours. These are presentation changes. We tried to stay close to familiar GOV.UK behaviour and patterns.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Where it runs</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The live service runs on Defra&rsquo;s <strong>Core Delivery Platform</strong> (CDP), the department&rsquo;s internal hosting platform. It is made up of a frontend service and a backend API, and data is stored in a <strong>MongoDB</strong> database managed by the platform. Sign-in and sessions need those services to be running and set up correctly.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Security</h2>
      <div class="govuk-inset-text">
        <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-margin-bottom-0">
          We have <strong>not</strong> run strong, independent security testing on the tool. For example we have not had a penetration test or a full threat-model exercise. We rely on sensible defaults from the GOV.UK and Defra service templates, the Core Delivery Platform, and on being careful about what data we hold.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="govuk-body govuk-!-margin-top-4">
        Access is limited. People sign in with a link sent to their Defra email address, and the service only works on the Defra network. In the service, only the person, their line manager and the Head of Design can see someone&rsquo;s GDaD evidence and scores.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        The service now holds GDaD evidence and scores, which are linked to people&rsquo;s pay. When it only helped designers find help, we thought the security risk was relatively low. Because it now holds pay-related information, <strong>that view needs reviewing</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        We are checking what data protection requires, including whether we need a data protection impact assessment (DPIA). If the role of the service keeps growing, we would expect more testing and clearer governance, and we might need to change how it is built.
      </p>

      <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Working in the open about this build</h2>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        Part of building this way was an <strong>experiment</strong>. We wanted to see if we could ship something useful quickly with AI help and a clear brief from a person. Similar work might once have needed several people over several weeks.
      </p>
      <p class="govuk-body">
        There are trade-offs: you can move fast, but you may do less deep review. You can explore ideas, but you may be less consistent than a larger team. Working in the open about those trade-offs helps us judge when this way of working is good enough — and when we need a different approach.
      </p>

      <p class="govuk-body">
        <a class="govuk-link govuk-link--no-visited-state" href="/about">Back to About Design help</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endblock %}
